-- RLS step 2c: table, column and function grants to `authenticated`.
-- (Trov #115; plan: docs/security/rls-step2-plan.md §1.2–§1.5, §6.1.
-- Generated by docs/security/rls-step2/gen_policies.py `grants`.)
--
-- What this does
-- --------------
-- Grants `authenticated` exactly the privileges the step-2b policies
-- (20261108094129_rls_policies.sql) cover, on 55 public tables, so the
-- coming user-scoped PostgREST client (a backend-minted JWT, iss
-- 'sapling-api', role authenticated) can reach its own rows:
--   * Bucket A/B (owned): the commands each table's own-row policies cover,
--     e.g. SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on notes; SELECT only on users,
--     user_cosmetics, user_roles, xp_events; SELECT/INSERT on feedback,
--     issue_reports; SELECT/INSERT on node_mastery_events (append-only).
--   * documents: SELECT, INSERT, DELETE, and UPDATE on the user-owned columns
--     only (file_name, category, summary, concept_notes, flashcards,
--     processed_at, deleted_at, extracted_text, shareability,
--     shareability_confidence, offering_id). NOT the index_* lease columns:
--     document_indexing owns those and runs as service_role, so a mis-scoped
--     indexer fails loudly instead of corrupting the lease.
--   * user_achievements: SELECT, and UPDATE (is_featured) only. Awards stay
--     server-side (achievement_service, service_role).
--   * Bucket C (shared): rooms S/I/U, room_members S/I/D, room_bans S/I/U/D,
--     room_activity S/I, room_reactions S/I/D; read-only room_summaries,
--     offering_concept_stats, offering_summary, course_chunks,
--     course_chunk_contributors, course_chunk_sources. room_messages is not
--     touched (step 2e).
--   * Bucket D (catalog): SELECT on the 12 catalog tables.
--   * Bucket E (service-only): nothing.
--   * EXECUTE on public.match_course_chunks and public.apply_mastery_delta,
--     which step 2a revoked from everyone but service_role. Both are SECURITY
--     INVOKER, so course_chunks / graph_nodes RLS still applies inside them.
--     pgvector's `<=>` (an extension member) was left executable by 2a.
-- tests/integration/test_rls_lockdown_db.py pins this list exactly (no more,
-- no less) and checks every granted command has a policy.
--
-- Why it is inert for the backend today
-- -------------------------------------
-- The backend reaches PostgREST as service_role (BYPASSRLS), and the
-- migration/oracle/test connections are `postgres`, the table owner. Nothing
-- mints an `iss: sapling-api` token until the `db_user_scope` flag ships in a
-- later PR, so no request resolves app_auth.uid() to a user yet.
--
-- Residual exposure (what this grant opens to tokens that DO exist today)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Supabase Auth signups are ON in staging and prod (Email provider; plan
-- Addendum), so anyone can obtain a GoTrue-issued `authenticated` JWT that
-- PostgREST accepts. For such a token app_auth.uid() is NULL (wrong iss, and
-- its sub is a uuid), so:
--   * every owner, parent, membership and enrollment policy denies, and the
--     catalog (bucket D), rooms and course_chunks policies require
--     app_auth.uid() IS NOT NULL. It reads ZERO rows from all 55 granted
--     tables, catalog included (verified table by table in a replayed
--     container: tests/integration/test_rls_policies_db.py). Every INSERT /
--     UPDATE WITH CHECK compares a column, or a parent row's owner, with
--     app_auth.uid(), so its writes fail too.
--   * it can call /rpc/match_course_chunks (returns no rows: the course_chunks
--     SELECT policy filters every row) and /rpc/apply_mastery_delta (returns
--     no row and changes nothing: graph_nodes RLS hides every node).
--   * what it can gain is metadata: under PostgREST's default OpenAPI mode
--     (follow-privileges) the root endpoint describes the tables a role holds
--     privileges on, so the table and column names of the 55 granted tables
--     may become visible to it. No row data. (Not checked on hosted
--     Supabase.)
--   * unchanged by this migration: room_messages. Where RLS is off on it
--     (local, CI, staging) both anon and authenticated keep the SELECT step
--     2a left them for Realtime, so a signed-up stranger can read staging's
--     room messages today, exactly as the anon key can. Production has RLS on
--     with zero policies there, so it reads nothing. Step 2e closes this.
-- Turning Supabase Auth signups off on both projects (plan Addendum) removes
-- the token class entirely; it is not required for row safety.
--
-- Idempotent: GRANT is a no-op when already held. The DO block returns early
-- on a bare Postgres without the `authenticated` role. A missing table,
-- column or function signature fails the migration (rolled back), which is
-- the right outcome for schema drift. No data is touched.
--
-- Reverse (by hand, as the migration role; revoking is a metadata change and
-- restores #807 + 2a's state, where authenticated held nothing on these
-- tables and could execute neither function). Run this BEFORE reversing 2b:
--   REVOKE SELECT ON public.users FROM authenticated;
--   REVOKE SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE ON public.user_profiles FROM authenticated;
--   REVOKE SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE ON public.user_settings FROM authenticated;
--   REVOKE SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON public.oauth_tokens FROM authenticated;
--   REVOKE SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON public.gradescope_credentials FROM authenticated;
--   REVOKE SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON public.enrollments FROM authenticated;
--   REVOKE SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON public.notes FROM authenticated;
